#: kpgpui.cpp:155
msgid "&Keep passphrase in memory"
msgstr ""

#: kpgpui.cpp:157
msgid ""
"<qt>"
"<p>When this option is enabled, the passphrase of your private key will be "
"remembered by the application as long as the application is running. Thus "
"you will only have to enter the passphrase once.</p>"
"<p>Be aware that this could be a security risk. If you leave your computer, "
"others can use it to send signed messages and/or read your encrypted "
"messages. If a core dump occurs, the contents of your RAM will be saved onto "
"disk, including your passphrase.</p>"
"<p>Note that when using KMail, this setting only applies if you are not "
"using gpg-agent. It is also ignored if you are using crypto plugins.</p></qt>"
msgstr ""

#: kpgpui.cpp:170
msgid "Always encr&ypt to self"
msgstr ""

#: kpgpui.cpp:172
msgid ""
"<qt>"
"<p>When this option is enabled, the message/file will not only be encrypted "
"with the receiver's public key, but also with your key. This will enable you "
"to decrypt the message/file at a later time. This is generally a good "
"idea.</p></qt>"
msgstr ""

#: kpgpui.cpp:181
msgid "&Show signed/encrypted text after composing"
msgstr ""

#: kpgpui.cpp:184
msgid ""
"<qt>"
"<p>"
"When this option is enabled, the signed/encrypted text will be shown in a "
"separate window, enabling you to know how it will look before it is sent. "
"This is a good idea when you are verifying that your encryption system "
"works.</p></qt>"
msgstr ""

#: kpgpui.cpp:190
msgid "Always show the encryption keys &for approval"
msgstr ""

#: kpgpui.cpp:193
msgid ""
"<qt>"
"<p>When this option is enabled, the application will always show you a list "
"of public keys from which you can choose the one it will use for encryption. "
"If it is off, the application will only show the dialog if it cannot find "
"the right key or if there are several which could be used. </p></qt>"
msgstr ""

